From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: The Playport authors <dev@playport.dev>
Date: Sat, 17 Oct 2026 19:30:57 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] Windows: shrink the iOS call-return stack to 4 MiB

Every emulator thread reserves its call-return stack in the 16 GiB FEX
host band, 16 MiB and two guard pages each. In the Rockstar Games
Launcher's Social Club tree, 204 threads held 3.3 GB of the band in
call-return stacks when it filled at 16,202 MB; FEX's next allocation in
a CEF renderer failed and the renderer died before Social Club showed
its window.

The stack is a return-address predictor: a pop compares the guest
address it saved and falls back to the lookup cache on a mismatch, so a
reset costs a prediction, never a result. Madeira raised it from 4 MiB
to 16 MiB for entries that SEH unwinds leak; ARM64EC's inline guard now
resets a leaking stack pointer at its live window, and that window, not
the reservation, bounds a leak. 4 MiB, upstream's size, frees 12 MiB
of the band per thread.

The window the guard keeps and the place a reset puts the pointer were
hard-coded for 16 MiB (base + 4 MiB, [2 MiB, 6 MiB), the dispatcher's
whole-stack test by >> 24) and repeated at each site. They are now
named in InternalThreadState.h and follow the size: the pointer starts
and resets at the middle (2 MiB), the guard keeps it in [1 MiB, 3 MiB),
and the dispatcher tests the whole 4 MiB. The thread-start pointer,
the access-violation reset, CompileBlock's [CALLRET_OOB] reset and the
[callret-chain] depth use the same offset.

WOW64 has no inline guard: a leaking 32-bit thread now walks 2 MiB, not
4 MiB, before CompileBlock's check resets it.

Linear: PLA-383
Class: upstream-bug
Evidence: docs/evidence/2026-10-17-callret-stack.md
Offered-upstream: no; the inline guard and the host band are the iOS port's
